                     ========================== if docker ps nodes detail not come =======================
condition: Your nodes are Ready, so the cluster is working. docker ps is empty because Kubernetes isn’t using Docker.
Since v1.24 it talks to containerd directly, and you configured containerd as the runtime in Step 5.
Containers started by Kubernetes live in containerd, and Docker has no knowledge of them. 
Docker only shows containers that Docker itself started, and you haven’t started any.

solutioin:

Kubernetes containers live in containerd, so the real docker command can’t show them. You can make docker ps work by pointing a docker alias at a Docker-compatible tool for containerd. There are two ways.

Option 1: nerdctl (closest to real Docker)

nerdctl copies Docker’s command syntax and works directly with containerd. Run this on each node where you want it:

VER=$(curl -s https://api.github.com/repos/containerd/nerdctl/releases/latest | grep tag_name | cut -d '"' -f4 | sed 's/v//')
curl -LO https://github.com/containerd/nerdctl/releases/download/v${VER}/nerdctl-${VER}-linux-amd64.tar.gz
sudo tar Cxzvf /usr/local/bin nerdctl-${VER}-linux-amd64.tar.gz



Test it:

sudo nerdctl --namespace k8s.io ps



The --namespace k8s.io part is required, because Kubernetes keeps its containers in that namespace.

Now make docker an alias for it:

echo "alias docker='sudo nerdctl --namespace k8s.io'" >> ~/.bashrc
source ~/.bashrc



After that, these work as you’d expect:
docker ps
docker images
docker logs <container-id>
docker exec -it <container-id> sh


Option 2: crictl alias (nothing to install)

crictl is already on your nodes. First set its socket once:

bash
printf "runtime-endpoint: unix:///run/containerd/containerd.sock\nimage-endpoint: unix:///run/containerd/containerd.sock\n" | sudo tee /etc/crictl.yaml
echo "alias docker='sudo crictl'" >> ~/.bashrc
source ~/.bashrc

Then docker ps, docker images and docker logs work. The output columns differ slightly from real Docker, and fewer commands are supported.

Things to know
The alias hides the real Docker command in your shell. To run real Docker (for example docker run for non-Kubernetes containers), use /usr/bin/docker, or remove the alias with unalias docker.
The alias works only when you type docker ... as your normal user. sudo docker ps will still call the real Docker.
Test it by creating pods with kubectl create deployment nginx --image=nginx --replicas=2, then run docker ps on the worker. You should see the nginx containers.

I wouldn’t switch Kubernetes itself back to Docker (it needs the extra cri-dockerd shim and a cluster rebuild). The aliases above give you the same docker ps view without that work.



=====================================================================================================================================
 Calico and Weave Net are both CNI plugins (Container Network Interface). They provide the pod network in a Kubernetes cluster.
=====================================================================================================================================



Why a cluster needs one

Kubernetes doesn’t ship with pod networking. Without a CNI plugin, pods can’t get IP addresses or talk to pods on other nodes, and nodes stay NotReady. That’s what you saw.

A CNI plugin does these jobs:

Gives every pod its own IP address
Lets pods on different nodes communicate with each other
Optionally enforces network policies (rules about which pods can talk to which)
Calico

Calico is maintained by Tigera and is one of the most widely used CNI plugins.

Routes pod traffic using standard IP routing (BGP or IP-in-IP/VXLAN tunnels)
Has strong network policy support for security
Scales well to large clusters
Actively maintained and tested on current Kubernetes versions
Weave Net

Weave Net was made by Weaveworks. It builds an overlay network that links the nodes together.

Simple to install, which is why many older tutorials use it
Includes basic network policy support
Can encrypt traffic between nodes
No longer maintained. Weaveworks shut down in 2024 and the project is archived, with v2.8.1 as the final release.
Comparison
	                       Calico                                 	Weave Net
Status	               Actively maintained	                  Archived, no updates
Network policies	      Advanced	                                 Basic
Performance and scale	  High                                  	Moderate
Works on Kubernetes 1.35  Yes (v3.31 tested)	             Uncertain, failed in your cluster
Good for new clusters	    Yes	                                            No


- Other options:
Cilium: a modern, popular choice that uses eBPF
Flannel: very simple, but has no network policies
For your cluster

Use Calico. It’s the safe choice for a new kubeadm cluster, and the install commands in my last message will get your nodes to Ready. The tutorial you’re following was written when Weave was still active, which is why it uses it.


